<chapter
 id="lecture07-10">
#title("Example: Game of Twenty-four")

#para("\

Given four integers n1, n2, n3 and n4, one chooses two and uses them
to produce a rational number r1 by applying either addition,
subtraction, multiplication or division; one mixes r1 with the
remaining two numbers and chooses two of them to produce a rational
number r2 by applying either addition, subtraction, multiplication or
division; one then takes r2 and the last remaining number to produce a
rational number r3 by applying addition, subtraction, multiplication,
or division; if there exists a way to make r3 equal 24, then (n1, n2,
n3, n4) is said to be a good quad. For instance, (10,10,4,4) is a good
quad since we have: (10*10-4)/4 = 24; (5,7,7,11) is a good quad since
we have: (5-11/7)*7 = 24. Game-of-24 is a game that determines whether
four given integers form a good quad or not. For a demo of
Game-of-24, please visit <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/07-10/CODE/Game-of-24.html\">this link</ulink>
(the demo is the JS version of the GC era).

")

#para("\

In the following presentation of this chapter, I intend to present an
implementation of Game-of-24 in pure functional style, solidifying
various functional programming concepts covered so far. Please pay
attention to the support for overloading in ATS, which helps make
the presented code significantly easier to access.\

")

#para("\

Instead of making direct use of rational numbers in the
implementation, I choose to use doubles (that is, floating point
numbers of double precision) to approaximate them.  The following
declarations introduce #dyncode("int2dbl") and #dyncode("dbl2int") for
casting integers to doubles and vice versa, respectively:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define
int2dbl g0int2float_int_double
\#define
dbl2int g0float2int_double_int
//
')

")

#para("\

In the case where four given integers are claimed to form a good quad,
we may want to see a proof to justify the claim. The type
#stacode("expr") is meant for providing such a proof: an expression
is either a number or the application of an operator to two expressions.
In the GC era, #stacode("expr") was a datatype. Here a datatype
with a non-nullary constructor is rejected: its values would be shared
(a subexpression is a part of many expressions in this program) and shared
heap-allocated values can only be reclaimed by a GC. A linear datavtype
would require the expressions to be copied each time they are shared.
The solution for shared data is to keep them in an #emphasis("arena"): the
nodes are allocated in a region (the global arena of the library
#filename("libats/arena")) and the program uses handles (which are not
linear) to them; the nodes are never freed individually, they all live
until the program exits (or until a region is freed, if a local region is
used instead of the global one). The tool #command("arenagen") (the file
#filename("utils/arenagen/arenagen.py") of the repository) generates the
encoding of an algebraic type in an arena from a specification of the
type:

<informalexample><programlisting>
type expr
  EXPRnum (double)
  EXPRbop (char, expr, expr)
</programlisting></informalexample>

The files #filename("game24_gen.hats") and #filename("game24_gen_dats.hats") of the
code of this chapter are the output of the command
#command("python3 arenagen.py --global sats game24.spec") and of the command with
#command("dats") in place of #command("sats"), respectively. The interface is as follows:

#sats2xhtml('\
abstype
expr_type = ptr
typedef
expr = expr_type

//
datavtype
expr_view =
  | EXPRnum of (double)
  | EXPRbop of (char, expr, expr)
//
fun
expr_encode(v: expr_view): expr
fun
expr_view(x: expr): expr_view
fun
expr_view_free(v: expr_view): void
fun
expr_tag(x: expr): int
fun
expr_EXPRnum(a0: double): expr
fun
expr_EXPRbop(a0: char, a1: expr, a2: expr): expr
fun
expr_is_EXPRnum(x: expr): bool
fun
expr_is_EXPRbop(x: expr): bool
//
')

The type #stacode("expr") is the type of handles (it is not linear and a
handle can be copied freely); the function #dyncode("expr_EXPRnum") and the
function #dyncode("expr_EXPRbop") construct an expression; and to look at the
content of an expression, the function #dyncode("expr_view") turns a handle
into a value of the linear type #stacode("expr_view") (a datavtype with the
constructors of the specification), which is used for pattern matching
and is consumed by the patterns written with the symbol #keycode("~").
In the following, the lists of expressions are linear lists of handles, and so all
of the functions of the previous chapters on lists apply.

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// the arena encoding of [expr] is generated from game24.spec
//
staload "./game24.sats"
\#include "./game24_gen_dats.hats"
//
vtypedef exprlst = List0_vt(expr)
//
')

For instance, the expression #dyncode("(10*10-4)/4") can be represented
as a value of the type #stacode("expr"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val myproof =
expr_EXPRbop(\'/\', expr_EXPRbop(\'-\', expr_EXPRbop(\'*\', expr_EXPRnum(10.0), expr_EXPRnum(10.0)), expr_EXPRnum(4.0)), expr_EXPRnum(4.0))
//
')

Note that values of the type #stacode("expr") is often referred as
abstract syntax trees.

")

#para("\

Given an expression (that is, an expr-value), the following function
#dyncode("eval_expr") returns the value of the expression:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
eval_expr(x0: expr): double
//
overload eval with eval_expr
//
implement
eval_expr(x0) =
(
case+ expr_view(x0) of
| ~EXPRnum(v0) => v0
| ~EXPRbop(opr, x1, x2) => let
    val v1 = eval_expr(x1)
    val v2 = eval_expr(x2)
  in
    case+ opr of
    | \'+\' => v1 + v2
    | \'-\' => v1 - v2
    | \'*\' => v1 * v2
    | \'/\' => v1 / v2
    | _(*unrecognized*) =>
      let val () = assertloc(false) in 0.0 end
  end
) (* end of [eval_expr] *)
//
')

")

#para("\

The following functions #dyncode("print_expr") and
#dyncode("fprint_expr") are needed for displaying an expression (that
is, an expr-value):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
print_expr : (expr) -> void
extern
fun
fprint_expr : (FILEref, expr) -> void
//
overload print with print_expr
overload fprint with fprint_expr
//

//
implement
print_expr(x0) =
fprint_expr(stdout_ref, x0)
//
implement
fprint_expr(out, x0) =
(
case+ expr_view(x0) of
| ~EXPRnum(v0) =>
  fprint(out, dbl2int(v0))
| ~EXPRbop(opr, x1, x2) =>
  fprint!(out, "(", x1, opr, x2, ")")
)
//
')

")

#para("\

The following functions are introduced for constructing abstract
syntax trees to represent the 4 arithmetic operations: addition,
subtraction, multiplication, and division:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
add_expr_expr
  : (expr, expr) -> expr
and
sub_expr_expr
  : (expr, expr) -> expr
and
mul_expr_expr
  : (expr, expr) -> expr
and
div_expr_expr
  : (expr, expr) -> expr
//
overload + with add_expr_expr
overload - with sub_expr_expr
overload * with mul_expr_expr
overload / with div_expr_expr
//

//
implement
add_expr_expr(x1, x2) = expr_EXPRbop(\'+\', x1, x2)
implement
sub_expr_expr(x1, x2) = expr_EXPRbop(\'-\', x1, x2)
implement
mul_expr_expr(x1, x2) = expr_EXPRbop(\'*\', x1, x2)
implement
div_expr_expr(x1, x2) = expr_EXPRbop(\'/\', x1, x2)
//
')

By overloading familiar symbols like #dyncode("+"), #dyncode("-"),
#dyncode("*"), and #dyncode("/"), I expect that the presented code
should be easier to access (even for someone who knows very little
about the programming language in which the code is written).

")

#para("\

When using floating point numbers, one often needs to
pay special attention equality test. For testing whether
the value of an expression equals a given integer, the following
function #dyncode("eq_expr_int") checks whether the absolute
difference between the value and the integer is less than
a tiny fraction #dyncode("EPSILON"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define EPSILON 1E-8
//
extern
fun
eq_expr_int(expr, int): bool
//
overload = with eq_expr_int
//
implement
eq_expr_int(x0, i0) = abs(eval_expr(x0) - i0) < EPSILON
//
')

I must say that choosing #dyncode("EPSILON") to be 10#sup("-8")
is an empirical decision (aiming at handling inputs that are small
integers (e.g., those between 0 and 100)).

")

#para("\

What is done so far can be thought of as providing basic setup
for the task of implementing Game-of-24. It is time for us to switch
to the algorithmic part of the task next.

")

#para("\

Given two expressions, the function #dyncode("combine_expr_expr")
returns a list consisting of every expression that can be constructed
by applying addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division to the
two given ones. The type #stacode("exprlst") is the type of the linear lists of
expressions, and the list that is returned is to be freed by the caller:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
combine_expr_expr(expr, expr): exprlst
//
overload combine with combine_expr_expr
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// [cons_if(b, x, res)] is [x :: res] if [b] is true or [res] otherwise
//
fun
cons_if
(b: bool, x: expr, res: exprlst): exprlst =
if b then list_vt_cons(x, res) else res
//
implement
combine_expr_expr
  (x1, x2) = res where
{
//
val res = list_vt_nil{expr}()
val res = list_vt_cons(x1+x2, res)
val res = list_vt_cons(x1-x2, res)
val res = list_vt_cons(x2-x1, res)
val res = list_vt_cons(x1*x2, res)
val res = cons_if(~(x2 = 0), x1/x2, res)
val res = cons_if(~(x1 = 0), x2/x1, res)
//
val res = list_vt_reverse(res)
//
} (* end of [combine_expr_expr] *)
//
')

Please note that the divisor must be non-zero if the division
operation is applied (the function #dyncode("cons_if") adds an expression
to a list only if its first argument is true).

")

#para("\

Let #stacode("task") be an alias for #stacode("List0_vt(expr)"). Given a
task-value #dyncode("xs") of the type #stacode("task"), we can choose
two expressions out of #dyncode("xs") to form a new expression (by
applying addition, subtraction, multiplication or division) and then
combine it with the rest to construct a new task-value. By calling
#dyncode("do_one") on #dyncode("xs"), we obtain all of the possible new task-values.
The function #dyncode("list_nchoose_rest") of the previous chapter returns a list of
pairs of linear lists, which is consumed by the function
#dyncode("auxlst") in the body of #dyncode("do_one"): the task #dyncode("xs")
is only borrowed, and the new tasks are fresh lists:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
//
vtypedef task = List0_vt(expr)
//
// the new tasks that are made of a list of expressions
// and a task [rest] (which is copied for each expression)
//
fun
tasks_make
(es: !List0_vt(expr), rest: !task): List0_vt(task) =
(
case+ es of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(e, es) =>
  list_vt_cons(list_vt_cons(e, list_vt_copy<expr>(rest)), tasks_make(es, rest))
)
//
extern
fun
do_one(xs: !task): List0_vt(task)
//
implement
do_one(xs) = let
//
// [yszs] is a list of pairs (a list of two expressions, the rest)
//
fun
auxlst
(yszs: List0_vt(@(task, task))): List0_vt(task) =
(
case+ yszs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(@(x1x2, rest), yszs) => let
    val x1 = list_get_at<expr>(x1x2, 0)
    val x2 = list_get_at<expr>(x1x2, 1)
    val es = combine(x1, x2)
    val res = tasks_make(es, rest)
    val () = list_vt_free<expr>(es)
    val () = list_vt_free<expr>(x1x2)
    val () = list_vt_free<expr>(rest)
  in
    list_vt_append<task>(res, auxlst(yszs))
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
)
//
in
  auxlst(list_nchoose_rest<expr>(xs, 2))
end // end of [do_one]
//
')

The function #dyncode("do_one_all") calls #dyncode("do_one") on each task of a
list of tasks and concatenates the results (it plays the role of
the combinator #dyncode("list0_mapjoin") of the GC era, which
mapped a function that returns a list over a list and flattened the result:
the tasks are linear here and the list of tasks is consumed), and the function
#dyncode("do_ones") repeats it:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// all the tasks that are obtained by calling [do_one] on each of the given
// tasks, which are consumed
//
fun
do_one_all
(xss: List0_vt(task)): List0_vt(task) =
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => let
    val res = do_one(xs)
    val () = list_vt_free<expr>(xs)
  in
    list_vt_append<task>(res, do_one_all(xss))
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
)
//
extern
fun
do_ones(n: int, xss: List0_vt(task)): List0_vt(task)
//
implement
do_ones(n, xss) =
if n >= 2
  then do_ones(n-1, do_one_all(xss)) else xss
//
')

Given four integers, the following function #dyncode("play_game")
returns a list of expressions that are proofs showing the four
integers being a good quad:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
play_game
( n1: int
, n2: int
, n3: int
, n4: int): exprlst
//
implement
play_game
  (n1, n2, n3, n4) = let
//
  val x1 = expr_EXPRnum(int2dbl(n1))
  val x2 = expr_EXPRnum(int2dbl(n2))
  val x3 = expr_EXPRnum(int2dbl(n3))
  val x4 = expr_EXPRnum(int2dbl(n4))
//
  val xs = $list_vt{expr}(x1, x2, x3, x4)
  val xss = do_ones(4, list_vt_cons(xs, list_vt_nil()))
//
  // only the tasks that consist of an expression
  // of the value 24 are kept (the tasks are consumed)
  fun
  auxlst
  (xss: List0_vt(task)): exprlst =
  (
    case+ xss of
    | ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
    | ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => let
        val-~list_vt_cons(x, xs) = xs
        val () = list_vt_free<expr>(xs)
      in
        if (x = 24)
          then list_vt_cons(x, auxlst(xss)) else auxlst(xss)
      end // end of [list_vt_cons]
  )
//
in
  auxlst(xss)
end // end of [play_game]
//
')

If we think of an option-value as a list of length at most 1, then the
function #dyncode("auxlst") in the body of #dyncode("play_game") behaves
just like a combinator that maps a function returning an option over a list and
keeps the contents of the options (the GC era had #dyncode("list0_mapopt") for this).
Note that four given integers
are considered a good quad if and only if the list returned by a call to
#dyncode("play_game") on them is non-empty, and that this list is to be freed.

")

#para("\

The code presented in this chapter targets C.  The code of the GC era could
also be compiled into JS for interpretation inside a
browser after a bit of <ulink
url=\"https://github.com/ats-lang/ats-lang.github.io/blob/master/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/07-10/CODE/Game-of-24-js.dats\">adaption</ulink>.
The arena is a library for C and the JS version has not been ported.

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/07-10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. The test in #filename("game24.dats") was
compiled with #command("patscc") (no flag is needed) and run with the address
sanitizer, which reports no leak: (10,10,4,4) and (5,7,7,11) are good quads and
(1,1,1,1) is not.

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture07-10"-->

